@page "/implementation-plan"

<PageTitle>Implementation Plan - OperationsFlow</PageTitle>

<div class="of-page">
    <PageHero Eyebrow="OPERATIONSFLOW / IMPLEMENTATION"
              Title="Implementation Plan"
              Subtitle="Where OperationsFlow is now, what Weeks 1–4 completed, and what remains before a real workplace deployment."
              BadgeText="Week 4 Pilot Implemented"
              BadgeClass="of-badge of-badge-success">
        <Actions>
            <a class="of-btn of-btn-secondary" href="/technical-overview">Technical Overview</a>
            <a class="of-btn of-btn-secondary" href="/integration-overview">Integration Overview</a>
            <a class="of-btn of-btn-secondary" href="/prototype-scope">Prototype Scope</a>
            <a class="of-btn of-btn-primary" href="/user-roles">User Roles</a>
        </Actions>
    </PageHero>

    <PurposeNote Title="Purpose" Icon="🧭">
        <p>
            This page explains how OperationsFlow moved from workflow MVP to SQL-backed local foundation and then to a working
            Microsoft 365 pilot implementation with OAuth2, Graph, SharePoint evidence storage, metadata writeback, and role mapping.
        </p>
    </PurposeNote>

    <TableCard Title="Implementation stages"
               Subtitle="Week 4 is now implemented as a single-tenant Microsoft 365 pilot, not just a future plan.">
        <ChildContent>
            <table class="table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Stage</th>
                        <th>Status</th>
                        <th>Result</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Week 1</td>
                        <td><span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Complete</span></td>
                        <td>MVP workflow shape, dashboard, seeded data, and core module layout.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Week 2</td>
                        <td><span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Complete</span></td>
                        <td>Reusable UI system, workflow pages, reports, data quality, activity, and portfolio review layer.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Week 3</td>
                        <td><span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Locked</span></td>
                        <td>Local/free production foundation with SQL-backed auth, roles, permissions, evidence workflow, reports, and storage abstraction.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Week 4</td>
                        <td><span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Pilot implemented</span></td>
                        <td>Microsoft OAuth2, account linking, Graph connection, SharePoint storage, metadata writeback, delete lifecycle, and configuration checks.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </ChildContent>
    </TableCard>

    <InfoPanel Title="Week 4 implementation blocks"
               Subtitle="The Microsoft 365 pilot was built in controlled blocks to avoid breaking the Week 3 foundation."
               Tone="success">
        <ChildContent>
            <div class="of-review-proof-grid">
                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 1</span>
                    <h3>Configuration foundation</h3>
                    <p>Safe Microsoft 365 configuration structure with no committed secrets.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 2</span>
                    <h3>Graph connection test</h3>
                    <p>Resolved Microsoft Graph, SharePoint site, and evidence document library.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 3</span>
                    <h3>SharePoint upload provider</h3>
                    <p>Uploaded evidence from OperationsFlow into SharePoint using the storage abstraction.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 4</span>
                    <h3>Metadata writeback</h3>
                    <p>Wrote OperationsFlow module, record, evidence, uploader, and notes metadata to SharePoint columns.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 5</span>
                    <h3>Document lifecycle</h3>
                    <p>Added direct Document Library parity, SharePoint delete lifecycle, current user activity, and UI polish.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="of-review-proof-card">
                    <span class="of-badge of-badge-success">Block 6</span>
                    <h3>Microsoft OAuth2 mapping</h3>
                    <p>Added Microsoft sign-in, local user mapping, and local-vs-Microsoft sign-in status display.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </ChildContent>
    </InfoPanel>

    <TableCard Title="Current architecture"
               Subtitle="The Week 4 pilot keeps clear separation between identity, authorization, storage, and workflow.">
        <ChildContent>
            <table class="table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Layer</th>
                        <th>Current implementation</th>
                        <th>Reason</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Identity</td>
                        <td>Local SQL login and Microsoft OAuth2 sign-in.</td>
                        <td>Supports offline/local demo and tenant-backed sign-in.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Account linking</td>
                        <td>ExternalLoginLinks maps Microsoft identity to LocalUser.</td>
                        <td>Keeps local application permissions stable even when identity provider changes.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Authorization</td>
                        <td>Local SQL roles and permission matrix.</td>
                        <td>OperationsFlow owns business permissions such as upload, delete, export, and manage users.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Storage</td>
                        <td>File storage abstraction with Local and SharePoint providers.</td>
                        <td>Same workflow pages can run in local mode or Microsoft 365 pilot mode.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Evidence</td>
                        <td>SharePoint files plus local DocumentAttachment records.</td>
                        <td>SharePoint stores the file; SQL stores workflow metadata and reporting links.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Audit</td>
                        <td>Activity Log records upload/delete/workflow events.</td>
                        <td>Reviewers can trace what changed, when, and by whom.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </ChildContent>
    </TableCard>

    <InfoPanel Title="Remaining production hardening"
               Subtitle="These items are outside the Week 4 portfolio pilot and would be needed before real customer production rollout."
               Tone="warning">
        <ChildContent>
            <ul class="of-mini-list">
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Secret management:</strong>
                    <span>Move secrets to user-secrets, environment variables, or a managed secret store. Rotate exposed/local test secrets.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Least privilege:</strong>
                    <span>Review Graph permissions and reduce broad permissions where practical.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Deployment:</strong>
                    <span>Host with HTTPS, production database, backup/restore, logging, and environment-specific config.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Account management:</strong>
                    <span>Add admin UI for creating users, linking/unlinking Microsoft accounts, and resetting local passwords.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Compliance:</strong>
                    <span>Define retention, backup, document deletion, audit review, and customer data policies.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="of-mini-list-item">
                    <strong>Monitoring:</strong>
                    <span>Add error logging, retry logic, sync health checks, and admin-visible integration failures.</span>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </ChildContent>
    </InfoPanel>

    <GuidanceNote Title="Positioning" Icon="✅" Tone="success">
        <p>
            The strongest honest label is: <strong>production pilot-ready single-tenant Microsoft 365 workflow system</strong>.
            It has a working local production foundation and a real Microsoft 365 pilot path, but it is not yet a fully hardened enterprise SaaS platform.
        </p>
    </GuidanceNote>
</div>